::PC_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L01_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1
Question:
Which given information points most directly to Law of Sines?
{
~A. SSS#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
=B. AAS or ASA with an opposite pair#Correct. Law of Sines depends on side-angle opposite pairs.
~C. SAS#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~D. Only one side#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L01_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1
Question:
Which given information points most directly to Law of Cosines?
{
~A. Two angles and one side#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. One opposite pair#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~C. Only angles#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
=D. SAS or SSS#Correct. Law of Cosines is used for SAS or SSS information.
}
::PC_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L01_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1
Question:
In a triangle, what does side b represent?
{
=A. The side opposite angle B#Correct. Lowercase side b is opposite uppercase angle B.
~B. The angle opposite side B#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~C. The longest side always#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
~D. The included angle#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L01_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1
Question:
What is the first step in a triangle application problem?
{
~A. Round all values#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. Assume a right triangle#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
=C. Identify the given sides and angles#Correct. Formula choice depends on the given sides and angles.
~D. Use every formula#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L01_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.1
Question:
Why can Law of Cosines work when no opposite pair is known?
{
~A. It ignores angles#This choice does not match the formula structure required by the lesson.
~B. It only works for right triangles#This reflects a common setup error; identify the given sides and angles first.
=C. It can use SAS or SSS information#Correct. Law of Cosines connects side lengths and an included angle or three sides.
~D. It requires a 90 degree angle#This does not connect the triangle information to the correct trigonometric relationship.
}
